---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { dataFor } from '../../lib/data';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';
import { cellViews, holdingTiles, HEAT_STEPS, partialYears } from '../../lib/holding-shared';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
const dataset = dataFor(currency).holdingPeriods;

const tiles = holdingTiles(dataset);
const cells = cellViews(dataset);
const { summary } = dataset;
const years = dataset.years.map((y) => y.year);
const firstYear = years[0];
const lastYear = years.at(-1);
const wholeYears = dataset.years.filter((y) => y.whole);
const partial = partialYears(dataset);
const partialList = partial.join(' and ');
const partialDates = dataset.years
  .filter((y) => !y.whole)
  .map((y) => `${y.year} runs ${y.basisDate} to ${y.closeDate}`)
  .join('; ');
const losing = summary.count - summary.positive;
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · holding periods"
  description="Every buy year against every sell year: what each hold returned, annualised, and how long you had to hold to never be down."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Holding periods</h1>

  <dl class="stat-grid">
    {
      tiles.map((tile) => (
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>{tile.label}</dt>
          <dd class={`num ${tile.tone}`}>{tile.value}</dd>
          <dd class="sub num">{tile.sub}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Bought at the start of, sold at the end of</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="risk-table heatmap holding-matrix">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">
          Annualised return for every hold from a buy year to a sell year. Rows are the year
          bought, columns the year sold; a cell's description gives the total return and the
          number of years held.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Buy \ sell</th>
            {
              years.map((year) => (
                <th scope="col" class="num">
                  {String(year).slice(2)}
                  {partial.includes(year) && <abbr title="not a whole calendar year">*</abbr>}
                </th>
              ))
            }
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            years.map((buyYear) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" class="num">
                  {buyYear}
                  {partial.includes(buyYear) && <abbr title="not a whole calendar year">*</abbr>}
                </th>
                {years.map((sellYear) => {
                  const cell = cells.get(`${buyYear}-${sellYear}`);
                  return cell === undefined ? (
                    // Not a hold, so not a gap in the data — an empty cell with
                    // no label, since a screen reader announcing "no data" here
                    // would be describing a triangle as a hole.
                    <td class="num blank" aria-hidden="true" />
                  ) : (
                    <td class={`num ${cell.heat}`} title={cell.title}>
                      <span class="visually-hidden">{cell.title}</span>
                      <span aria-hidden="true">{cell.label}</span>
                    </td>
                  );
                })}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="method-note">
      Each cell is one hold: bought at the start of the row's year, sold at the end of the
      column's. The figure is the <strong>annualised</strong> rate, because that is the only one
      comparable across the grid — a 300% total is extraordinary over one year and ordinary over
      ten — and each cell's description carries the total return and the number of years held. The
      lower-left half is empty because selling before you bought is not a hold.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      A year is priced from the last close of the previous December to the last close of its own,
      which is not the obvious choice and is the one that reconciles: the diagonal of this grid is
      exactly the yearly total the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${currency === 'usd' ? '' : 'gbp/'}cycles/`}>halving cycles</a> heatmap
      publishes for the same year. Anchoring on the first close of the buy year instead would put
      the two pages a few days out from each other, with nothing saying which to believe.
      {partial.length > 0 && (
        <>
          {' '}Both ends of the history are truncated, and the grid marks them with an asterisk:
          {' '}{partialDates}. A hold bought or sold in {partialList} is anchored on those dates
          rather than on a January or a December, and each cell's description names the date rather
          than saying "end of" a year that has not ended. Their own single-year holds run under
          {' '}{dataset.minAnnualiseDays} days and carry no annualised rate at all — below a year
          that figure is an extrapolation, and extrapolating {firstYear}'s gave +7,701% a year.
        </>
      )}
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Colour is fixed to the rate, not to this dataset's range, so the same shade means the same
      magnitude on every refresh: bands break at {HEAT_STEPS.join('%, ')}% a year either side of
      zero. The negative side uses the same numbers even though nothing here reaches −{HEAT_STEPS[2]}%,
      because an asymmetric scale would make a −60% and a +60% look like different magnitudes.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      A hold's length is counted in calendar years: bought at the start of {firstYear + 1} and sold
      at the end of {firstYear + 2} is two years, because the anchors are a December apart. The two
      asterisked years are the exception — a hold touching one is shorter than its label by however
      much of that year is missing, which is why each cell states its own duration in days or
      years rather than leaving you to infer it from the two headings.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      What this cannot tell you: it describes {wholeYears.length} whole years of one asset that
      existed throughout them, which is the definition of a survivorship-shaped sample. "Every
      {' '}{summary.safeYears}-year hold ended up" is a fact about {firstYear}–{lastYear} and not a
      property of the asset — {losing} of {summary.count} holds here did end down, and the longest
      losing one ran {summary.longestLosing?.days} days. Returns are price returns: no fees, no
      taxes, no dividends, and a single purchase rather than the drip the{' '}
      <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${currency === 'usd' ? '' : 'gbp/'}dca/`}>DCA page</a> models.{fxNote(currency)}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
